  "verbalization": "Phytolaccoside B is shown to have a target relationship with Ephrin type-A receptor 3 through multiple pathways, with varying levels of confidence. The compound is linked to the mitogen-activated protein kinase kinase kinase 13, which is in turn targeted by a metabolite that ultimately targets Ephrin type-A receptor 3. Similarly, Phytolaccoside B is also associated with Serine/threonine-protein kinase MARK1 and Serine/threonine-protein kinase ULK2, which are both targeted by the same metabolite and also lead to Ephrin type-A receptor 3. These findings suggest that Phytolaccoside B may act through a shared metabolic pathway to influence Ephrin type-A receptor 3. The evidence is consistent, with the metabolite serving as a common intermediary in the relationship between Phytolaccoside B and the receptor. The strength of the evidence is supported by multiple independent pathways, but the exact mechanism by which Phytolaccoside B interacts with Ephrin type-A receptor 3 remains partially uncertain, particularly regarding the specific molecular interactions and the role of the metabolite in this process."
